course: money
lesson: 31
title: The lemonade stand: cost, price, profit and loss
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every business, from a lemonade stand to a giant company, works on one simple sum: money in, minus money spent, equals profit, and if the answer is below zero, it is a loss.
explain: Picture Sam and Ava's stand: lemons, sugar and cups cost £5, and they sell 20 cups at 50p, so £10 comes in; Ava shouts "We made £10!", but Sam is right, they only made £10 − £5 = £5.
explain: The next day it rains, they sell only 6 cups, which is £3, and they still spent £5, so they lose £2; that is the risk of running your own business.
explain: The price matters too: at £2 a cup hardly anyone buys, at 50p lots do, and when the kid next door sells at 40p, customers drift away unless your lemonade is better.
explain: Good businesses keep records of money in and out, agree how to share profit before they start, and do not buy more stock than they can use.
fact: Cost is the money spent to make something; revenue, or takings, is the money from selling; profit is revenue minus cost.
fact: Selling 20 cups at 50p brings in 20 × 50p = £10; with £5 of costs, the profit is £10 − £5 = £5.
fact: A loss is when costs are bigger than the money made: 6 cups at 50p is £3, and £3 − £5 = −£2, a loss of £2.
fact: Risk is the chance of losing; weather, luck and customers can change how much a business sells.
fact: If the price is too high, fewer people buy: 3 cups at £2 bring £6, but 20 cups at 50p bring £10.
fact: Competition means other businesses selling the same thing; when a rival sells cheaper, sales can drop, like from 20 cups to 12, which is 12 × 50p = £6.
fact: Adding a new product adds its own cost and profit: 10 biscuits at 30p bring £3, and if the pack cost £1, the biscuits make £2 profit.
fact: Quality is how good something is, and reputation is what people think of you; bad lemonade dropped sales from 20 cups to 8, which is 12 fewer.
fact: Accounts are records of money in and out: costs of £2 + £1 + £2 = £5 and takings of £12 give a profit of £7.
fact: Many people mix up takings and profit; profit is only what is left after costs.
fact: Partners should agree how to share profit before they start; a profit of £8 shared 50-50 is £4 each.
fact: Stock is the things a business keeps to sell or use; 20 lemons at 20p cost £4, and if only 12 are used, the 8 that go bad waste 8 × 20p = £1.60.
exercise: Sam and Ava sell 20 cups at 50p each. How much money comes in? | £10 | £20 | £1 | £5
exercise: They spent £5 and took £10. What is their profit? | £5 | £10 | £15 | £50
exercise: What is profit? | The money left after paying all the costs | All the money that comes in from selling | The money spent on lemons and cups | The price written on the sign
exercise: On the rainy day they sell 6 cups at 50p. How much comes in? | £3 | £6 | £30 | £2
exercise: They took £3 but spent £5. What happened? | They made a loss of £2 | They made a profit of £2 | They made a profit of £8 | They broke even exactly
exercise: At £2 a cup they might sell 3 cups; at 50p they sell 20. Which price brings in more money? | 50p, with £10 instead of £6 | £2, with £6 instead of £3 | £2, with £40 instead of £10 | Both bring in exactly the same
exercise: The kid next door sells at 40p and Sam and Ava now sell 12 cups at 50p. How much do they take? | £6 | £10 | £4.80 | £12
exercise: What is competition in business? | Other businesses selling the same thing | A race between the two people in a business | A prize given to the best lemonade | A rule that prices must be the same
exercise: They sell 10 biscuits at 30p and the pack cost £1. What profit do the biscuits make? | £2 | £3 | £1 | £30
exercise: Sales drop from 20 cups to 8 after the lemonade tastes sour. How many fewer cups? | 12 | 28 | 8 | 10
exercise: Why do sales drop when people say the lemonade is gross? | Quality matters, and word spreads, so customers stay away | Sour lemonade is against the rules in parks | People only buy lemonade on sunny days | The price goes up when the taste is bad
exercise: Sam's notebook says lemons £2, sugar £1, cups £2, and takings £12. What is the profit? | £7 | £5 | £12 | £17
exercise: What are accounts? | Records of money coming in and going out | Stories about how the business started | The names of all the customers | The money hidden in a secret box
exercise: Ava says, "We made £10!" They spent £5. What did they really make? | £5 profit, because takings are not profit | £10 profit, because that is what came in | £15 profit, because you add the costs | £0, because they spent money
exercise: They split a profit of £8 50-50. How much does each get? | £4 | £8 | £16 | £2
exercise: Sam did more work and wants more. Why is that a problem now? | They agreed 50-50 before, and changing the deal later causes rows | The law says profits can never be shared | Sam is not allowed any profit at all | 50-50 means Sam gets nothing
exercise: They bought 20 lemons at 20p and used 12. How much money went bad with the rest? | £1.60 | £2.40 | £4 | 80p
test: Ava shouts, "We made a tenner, we're rich, slay!" ("Slay" means doing amazing.) Sam says, "We spent a fiver, though." What's the real profit? | £5 | £10 | £15 | £50
test: What would happen if Sam and Ava charged £5 a cup? | Hardly anyone would buy, and they could take less money | Everybody would buy more, because it's fancier | They'd make £100 for sure | The lemons would get cheaper to buy
test: A Scouse customer says, "That lemonade's boss, la, but 50p's a bit much when yer man next door's doin' 40p." ("Boss" means great; "la" means mate.) What's going on? | Competition: a rival sells cheaper, so some customers move | Quality: the lemonade is terrible and nobody buys | Stock: they have run out of lemons and cups | Profit: they have made too much money already
test: Your mate says, "If it rains you can't lose money, you just sell less." They spent £5 and sold 6 cups at 50p. What's true? | They lost £2, so you can lose money | They made £3 profit, so no loss | They broke even, so nothing changed | They made £5, because costs don't count
test: In a game, your lemonade stand earns money even with no customers, as long as the sun is out. What's wrong? | In real life money only comes in when customers buy | Real stands earn money from the sun shining | Real stands are paid by the council per hour | Nothing, real stands earn while nobody buys
test: Sam keeps a notebook: "Lemons £2, sugar £1, cups £2 — oh, and 50p for a sign — takings £12." What's the profit now? | £6.50 | £7 | £7.50 | £5.50
test: Ava wants to split the £8 profit 50-50, then Sam says, "I did more, gimme £6." ("Gimme" means give me.) If they had agreed 75-25 at the start, what would Sam get? | £6 | £4 | £2 | £8
test: A Canadian kid at the stand says, "Your lemonade's sour, eh, I'm going to Tim's." (Tim Hortons is a big coffee chain in Canada.) What does this show? | Quality matters: bad taste sends customers to rivals | Canadians never buy lemonade from kids | Price is the only thing that matters | Sour lemonade always sells better
test: They sell 10 biscuits at 30p. Sam says, "Biscuits made us £3 profit." The pack cost £1. Is he right? | No, £3 is the takings; profit is £2 | Yes, takings and profit are the same thing | No, the biscuits made a loss of £1 | No, the profit is £4, adding the pack
test: They buy 20 lemons at 20p but use only 12. Next time, how many should they buy to waste none, if they sell the same? | 12 | 20 | 8 | 32
test: What would happen to the profit if they sold 20 cups at 50p but the costs went up to £8? | Profit would fall to £2 | Profit would rise to £18 | Profit would stay at £5 | They would make a loss of £8
exam: It is given that the costs of an enterprise amount to £5 and its revenue to £10. Determine the profit. | £5 | £10 | £15 | £50
exam: Ascertain the outcome of trading where six units are sold at fifty pence each, whereas costs of £5 have been incurred. | A loss of £2 | A profit of £2 | A profit of £3 | A loss of £5
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between takings and profit? | Profit is the sum remaining after costs have been deducted from takings | Takings and profit are identical in every respect | Profit is the sum of takings and costs together | Takings are the sum remaining after profit has been deducted
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the partners in an enterprise should agree upon the division of profit before trading commences. | The avoidance of disputes once the profit is known | Compliance with a requirement that all profit be paid as tax | The prevention of any profit from being made | The determination of the price of the goods sold
